{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792442169,"nonce":"AP+jcyM+ExU+PB6/+wWHwfnAtOE=","message":"Where the graphics memory went: a frame of RayDE began about 93 render passes, 3,077 in 34 submits. The record list had 48 layers, from the code editor's row slots, gutter slots and fold buttons, which were shown and hidden with opacity 1 and 0. blinc_abi draws a group at opacity under 1 with more than one painted node into a layer (a clear, a render and a composite over a texture of the window's size), and it does so at opacity 0 too, with all the records still in it. Each pass held about 2.4 MB of driver memory until the frame finished, so a frame in flight held 230 MB or more, and the amount grew with the window's size (peak 191 MB at 640x400 and 420 MB at 1280x800, logical, at 2x).\n\nFixed in the editor: a slot with no row, and the fold slot of a row that opens no fold, are `visibility: hidden`, which draws nothing and makes no records. After it a frame has 0 layers and 1 pass; the graphics peak through ten tab opens was 7 MB (it was 248 to 633 MB). `EditorLayersPixels` holds it (83 layers before, with a few lines in a tall window).\n\nblinc_abi still makes a layer for a group at opacity 0: it should skip the subtree. That is blinc_abi's, not changed here; any other app that fades a group to nothing with two or more painted nodes pays the same.\n\nAlso found and fixed this round: the command center in RayDE kept a pointer hook for good, which stops the window sleeping through moves in a still region (RayDE's CommandCenter.hx, uncommitted in its checkout); and the minimap's width, taken from the text's width, swapped between two sizes in narrow windows and laid out and drew each frame (a fifth of the editor's width now; the window prints a warning when its layout changes on 90 turns with nothing to cause it).","files":null}]}